/// A NAMED SHARED SECTION — a block of bytes two processes both map, addressed by name.
///
/// One process publishes a section of a fixed size under a name; another adopts the same name and sees
/// the same bytes. That is how a DebugStream trace travels: the traced child writes
/// fixed-size records into a ring inside the section, and the process watching it decodes what it finds
/// there. Nothing about the ring's layout is here — this file is the door to the bytes.
///
///     var segment = try SharedSegment.create("maxon-trace-1", bytes: 65536)
///     try segment.writeWord(0, value: 1)
///     let first = try segment.readWord(0)
///     segment.close()
///
/// Forgetting `close()` leaks the mapping AND keeps the section alive: a Win32 section object outlives
/// its handle for as long as any view of it is mapped, and a POSIX one outlives every descriptor and
/// every mapping until its publisher removes its name — until the next reboot. On POSIX a section left
/// behind by a publisher that exited without `close()` is adopted by the next `create` of its name.
///
/// Available on `x64-windows` (Win32 section objects), on `arm64-macos` (`shm_open` + `mmap`) and on both
/// Linux lanes (a `/dev/shm` file + `mmap`), the same targets the DebugStream host primitives reach. A call
/// site on any other target is refused at compile time.
///
/// @category System
/// How many bytes a section holds. Never zero — `CreateFileMappingA` reads a zero maximum size as "as
/// large as the backing file", which a page-file-backed section does not have, and `mmap` refuses a zero
/// length outright — and never wider than the DWORD the create call carries it in.
public typealias SegmentByteCount = int(1 to u32.max)

/// Where inside the mapping an accessor reads or writes. BYTES, never a word index: the records a
/// section carries are laid out by a format neither process owns, so a scaled offset would put one side's
/// reads a stride away from the other's writes.
public typealias SegmentOffset = int(0 to u32.max)

/// One 64-bit word of a mapping's contents. Signed and full-width because a section holds whatever its
/// format puts there, and nothing here interprets it.
public typealias SegmentWord = int(i64.min to i64.max)

// One past the last byte an access touches: an offset plus a byte count, so it can reach twice the widest
// either one is.
typealias SegmentEnd = int(0 to 8589934590)

// The opaque machine words the two host calls answer — a section HANDLE and the base address of a view.
typealias SegmentHandle = int(0 to u64.max)
typealias SegmentAddress = int(0 to u64.max)

typealias ByteArray = Array with Byte

// The answer both host calls give for a failure. Zero on every lane: Win32 answers NULL, and a POSIX lane
// answers descriptor 0 — never a real one, because fd 0 is standard input and the runtime mints no handle
// there. It is also what a teardown passes for the half a failed create never made.
let NoSection = 0

// The runtime's `SharedSegmentAnswer`s, pinned against these by `checkSharedMemoryAnswers` on every compile.
let NameAlreadyPublished = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF as SegmentHandle
let NotYetPublished = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE as SegmentHandle

let MostPublishOrAdoptAttempts = 1000
let PublishOrAdoptRetryMs = 1

// How many bytes one `SegmentWord` occupies in the mapping.
let SegmentWordBytes = 8

// The longest name `create` publishes, and the three bytes no name may hold — see `requirePortableName`.
let PortableSegmentNameBytes = 31
let SlashByte = 0x2F
let BackslashByte = 0x5C
let NulByte = 0

/// Why a section could not be opened or accessed. The two open arms are separate because the two host
/// calls fail for different reasons and a caller can act on the difference: a create fails on a name
/// collision with an incompatible section or on a size the system will not back, a map on address space.
/// `invalidName` is a name some lane cannot publish, refused before any host call (see `create`).
/// `outOfBounds` is an accessor whose bytes would reach past the end of the mapping.
public union SharedMemoryError implements Error
	createFailed
	mapFailed
	invalidName
	outOfBounds
end 'SharedMemoryError'

/// A named section, mapped into this process.
public type SharedSegment
	// The section object, and the base of this process's view of it. Both are needed to tear the mapping
	// down — see `close`.
	var handle as SegmentHandle
	var base as SegmentAddress

	// How large the mapping is. Kept because the teardown needs it on a lane whose unmap takes a length,
	// and the OS offers no way to read a view's extent back off its base.
	let bytes as SegmentByteCount

	// The name the section is published under, kept so `segmentName()` can answer it: the OS offers no way
	// to read a section's name back off its handle, and the name is what a second process needs.
	var name as String

	// Latched by `close()` so a second call is a no-op, the contract `StreamingSubprocess.release` states.
	var closed as bool

	let publisher as bool

	/// Publish a section of `bytes` bytes under `name` and map it into this process, or adopt the section
	/// already published under `name` and map `bytes` of it.
	///
	/// Two processes creating one name at once both succeed: one publishes and the other adopts, retrying
	/// for about a second while the publisher sizes the section. Throws `createFailed` when the published
	/// section is smaller than `bytes` or the host refuses the name, and `invalidName` for a name that is
	/// empty, longer than 31 bytes, or holds a `/`, a `\` or a NUL byte — on every target, including one
	/// whose host would have accepted it.
	public static function create(name String, bytes SegmentByteCount) returns SharedSegment throws SharedMemoryError
		try requirePortableName(name)

		for _ in 0 upto MostPublishOrAdoptAttempts 'eachAttempt'
			let published = try Self.attempt(SegmentRoad.publish, name: name, bytes: bytes)

			match published 'published'
				opened(segment) then return segment
				notOpenedYet then break 'published'
			end 'published'

			let adopted = try Self.attempt(SegmentRoad.adopt, name: name, bytes: bytes)

			match adopted 'adopted'
				opened(segment) then return segment
				notOpenedYet then sleep(PublishOrAdoptRetryMs)
			end 'adopted'
		end 'eachAttempt'

		throw SharedMemoryError.createFailed
	end 'create'

	/// Publish a new section of exactly `bytes` bytes under `name` and map it into this process. Throws
	/// `createFailed` when `name` is already published, so the section is always a fresh one, and
	/// `invalidName` for a name `create` refuses.
	public static function publish(name String, bytes SegmentByteCount) returns SharedSegment throws SharedMemoryError
		try requirePortableName(name)
		let published = try Self.attempt(SegmentRoad.publish, name: name, bytes: bytes)

		return match published 'published'
			opened(segment) gives segment
			notOpenedYet throws SharedMemoryError.createFailed
		end 'published'
	end 'publish'

	static function attempt(road SegmentRoad, name String, bytes SegmentByteCount) returns SegmentAttempt throws SharedMemoryError
		let raw = match road 'call'
			publish gives __Builtins.sharedMemoryCreate(name.cstr(), bytes)
			adopt gives __Builtins.sharedMemoryAdopt(name.cstr(), bytes)
		end 'call'

		if raw == NoSection 'refused'
			throw SharedMemoryError.createFailed
		end 'refused'

		if raw == NameAlreadyPublished or raw == NotYetPublished 'notYet'
			return SegmentAttempt.notOpenedYet
		end 'notYet'

		return SegmentAttempt.opened(try mapped(raw, name: name, bytes: bytes, publisher: road == SegmentRoad.publish))
	end 'attempt'

	static function mapped(rawHandle SegmentHandle, name String, bytes SegmentByteCount, publisher bool) returns SharedSegment throws SharedMemoryError
		let rawBase = __Builtins.sharedMemoryMap(rawHandle, bytes)
		var segment = SharedSegment{handle: rawHandle, base: rawBase, bytes: bytes, name: name, closed: false, publisher: publisher}

		if rawBase == NoSection 'mapFailed'
			segment.close()
			throw SharedMemoryError.mapFailed
		end 'mapFailed'

		return segment
	end 'mapped'

	// A name one lane cannot publish is refused on all of them, so a program that runs on one host does not
	// fail on another with a `createFailed` that names no cause. The length is Darwin's `PSHMNAMLEN`, the
	// smallest limit of the lanes; a `/` is a directory under `/dev/shm`, a `\` a Win32 object namespace, and
	// a NUL would end the host's name short of the one `segmentName()` answers.
	static function requirePortableName(name String) throws SharedMemoryError
		if name.byteLength() == 0 or name.byteLength() > PortableSegmentNameBytes 'badLength'
			throw SharedMemoryError.invalidName
		end 'badLength'

		for byte in name.bytes() 'eachByte'
			if byte == SlashByte or byte == BackslashByte or byte == NulByte 'reserved'
				throw SharedMemoryError.invalidName
			end 'reserved'
		end 'eachByte'
	end 'requirePortableName'

	/// The name this section is published under — what a second process maps it by.
	public function segmentName() returns String
		return name
	end 'segmentName'

	/// The 64-bit word at `offset` bytes into the mapping, read with an acquire load: once it returns a
	/// value another process wrote with `writeWord`, every write that process made before it is visible.
	/// Throws `outOfBounds` when the word would reach past the end of the mapping.
	public function readWord(offset SegmentOffset) returns SegmentWord throws SharedMemoryError
		try requireInside(offset, byteCount: SegmentWordBytes)
		return __Builtins.sharedMemoryReadWord(base, offset)
	end 'readWord'

	/// Write `value` as a 64-bit word at `offset` bytes into the mapping, with a release store: every write
	/// this process made before it is visible to a process that reads the word with `readWord`. Throws
	/// `outOfBounds` when the word would reach past the end of the mapping.
	public function writeWord(offset SegmentOffset, value SegmentWord) throws SharedMemoryError
		try requireInside(offset, byteCount: SegmentWordBytes)
		__Builtins.sharedMemoryWriteWord(base, offset, value)
	end 'writeWord'

	/// `byteCount` bytes of the mapping starting at `offset`, as a fresh independent array. Throws
	/// `outOfBounds` when the range would reach past the end of the mapping.
	///
	/// It COPIES rather than viewing: the bytes belong to a section another process may be writing, and a
	/// view would answer differently on each read and would outlive `close()`.
	public function copyOut(offset SegmentOffset, byteCount SegmentByteCount) returns ByteArray throws SharedMemoryError
		try requireInside(offset, byteCount: byteCount)
		return ByteArray.init(__Builtins.sharedMemoryCopyOut(base, offset, byteCount))
	end 'copyOut'

	// The host accessors read and write raw memory at `base + offset` and check nothing, so the mapping's
	// extent is enforced here, where `bytes` is known.
	function requireInside(offset SegmentOffset, byteCount SegmentByteCount) throws SharedMemoryError
		if (offset as SegmentEnd) + (byteCount as SegmentEnd) > (bytes as SegmentEnd) 'pastTheEnd'
			throw SharedMemoryError.outOfBounds
		end 'pastTheEnd'
	end 'requireInside'

	/// Unmap this process's view and release the section, and withdraw its name if this segment published
	/// it. Idempotent.
	///
	/// Every step is needed: a section object stays alive while any view of it is mapped, so releasing the
	/// handle alone would keep the section and its address range for the life of the process — and on a
	/// POSIX host the NAME outlives all of them, so a published segment closed without withdrawing it stays
	/// addressable to any later process until the machine is restarted. An adopter leaves the name to its
	/// publisher, whose other adopters still map it by that name.
	public function close()
		if closed 'alreadyClosed'
			return
		end 'alreadyClosed'

		if publisher 'withdrawTheName'
			__Builtins.sharedMemoryClose(handle, base, bytes, name.cstr())
		end 'withdrawTheName' else 'keepTheName'
			__Builtins.sharedMemoryRelease(handle, base, bytes)
		end 'keepTheName'

		closed = true
	end 'close'
end 'SharedSegment'

enum SegmentRoad
	publish
	adopt
end 'SegmentRoad'

union SegmentAttempt
	opened(segment SharedSegment)
	notOpenedYet
end 'SegmentAttempt'
